UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 24, Lesson 2: Designing a new game', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Designing a new game uses what we know about skills, rules, safety, fairness and inclusion. A game design needs a name, a goal, equipment, space, rules, a scoring system, safety rules and a way to adjust the game for different abilities. Test the game, ask for feedback and revise.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite two rules and one adjustment for a new game that uses throwing and catching so that everyone can join in.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRules: pass before you shoot; no throwing at people. Adjustment: use a lighter ball or shorter distances. These make the game safe, fair and inclusive.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking a good game has the most complicated rules. Simple, clear rules usually work best.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDesign and write the rules for a new game for 4 to 6 players. Include a name, goal, equipment, space, rules, scoring, safety rules and two adjustments. Play it with family and write down one change after testing.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Designing a new game uses what we know about skills, rules, safety, fairness and inclusion. A game design needs a name, a goal, equipment, space, rules, a scoring system, safety rules and a way to adjust the game for different abilities. Test the game, ask for feedback and revise.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Design and write the rules for a new game for 4 to 6 players. Include a name, goal, equipment, space, rules, scoring, safety rules and two adjustments. Play it with family and write down one change after testing. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using designing a game on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules.. Correction: Rules: pass before you shoot; no throwing at people.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules. Simple, clear rules usually work best. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating designing a game on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with designing a game, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about designing a game with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for designing a game. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for designing a game. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Rules: pass before you shoot","Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Write two rules and one adjustment for a new game that uses throwing and catching so that everyone can join in. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for designing a game; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Rules: pass before you shoot","Careful use of a game goal and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules","Designing a new game uses what we know about skills, rules, safety, fairness and inclusion"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with designing a game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a game goal and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules","Name a game goal and explain why it matters only, without linking it to designing a game","Apply designing a game to new material and name the a game goal and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies designing a game with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on designing a game. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules","Rules: pass before you shoot","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for designing a game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with designing a game","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about designing a game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Designing a new game uses what we know about skills, rules, safety) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example design ''Space Raiders'': 2 teams of 3, soft balls, a 12 by 12 metre area with 4 hoops","Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about designing a game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for designing a game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules","Name a game goal and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Design and write the rules for a new game for 4 to 6 players","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies designing a game to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about designing a game with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating designing a game on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using designing a game on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for designing a game. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with designing a game, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules.. Correction: Rules: pass before you shoot; no throwing at people.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w24-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise designing a game.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use designing a game with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a game goal and explain why it matters.","I can practise scoring independently on a new prompt.","I can notice adjustments needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Designing a new game uses what we know about skills, rules, safety, fairness and inclusion.","options":["Designing a new game uses what we know about skills, rules, safety, fairness and inclusion","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about designing a game with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample design ''Space Raiders'': 2 teams of 3, soft balls, a 12 by 12 metre area with 4 hoops. Goal: bounce-pass the ball into a hoop. Rules: no contact, three steps with the ball, every player touches the ball. Scoring: 1 point per hoop. Safety: bibs and no running into hoops. Adjustment: hoops can be moved closer.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite two rules and one adjustment for a new game that uses throwing and catching so that everyone can join in.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRules: pass before you shoot; no throwing at people. Adjustment: use a lighter ball or shorter distances. These make the game safe, fair and inclusive.","explain":"Designing a new game uses what we know about skills, rules, safety, fairness and inclusion. A game design needs a name, a goal, equipment, space, rules, a scoring system, safety rules and a way to adjust the game for different abilities. Test the game, ask for feedback and revise.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules. Simple, clear rules usually work best. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Parts of a game design.","caption":"Include every part.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Design</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">name, goal, rules, scoring</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safety</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">no contact, bibs</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example design ''Space Raiders'': 2 teams of 3, soft balls, a 12 by 12 metre area with 4 hoops. Goal: bounce-pass the ball Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example design ''Space Raiders'': 2 teams of 3, soft balls, a 12 by 12 metre area with 4 hoops. Goal: bounce-pass the ball Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about designing a game with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample design ''Space Raiders'': 2 teams of 3, soft balls, a 12 by 12 metre area with 4 hoops. Goal: bounce-pass the ball into a hoop. Rules: no contact, three steps with the ball, every player touches the ball. Scoring: 1 point per hoop. Safety: bibs and no running into hoops. Adjustment: hoops can be moved closer. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Adjusting a game for all.","caption":"Adjust so all can play.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Adjust</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">closer hoops</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Include</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lighter ball</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about designing a game with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking a good game has the most complicated rules. Simple, clear rules usually work best. Here is a more helpful example: Example design ''Space Raiders'': 2 teams of 3, soft balls, a 12 by 12 metre area with 4 hoops. Goal: bounce-pass the ball into a hoop. Rules: no contact, three steps with the ball, every player touches the ball. Scoring: 1 point per hoop. Safety: bibs and no running into hoops. Adjustment: hoops can be moved closer.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRules: pass before you shoot; no throwing at people. Adjustment: use a lighter ball or shorter distances. These make the game safe, fair and inclusive.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking a good game has the most complicated rules. Simple, clear rules usually work best. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about designing a game with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDesigning a new game uses what we know about skills, rules, safety, fairness and inclusion. A game design needs a name, a goal, equipment, space, rules, a scoring system, safety rules and a way to adjust the game for different abilities. Test the game, ask for feedback and revise. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 24, Lesson 2: Designing a new game","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Games and sports: teamwork and fair play","week":24,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample design ''Space Raiders'': 2 teams of 3, soft balls, a 12 by 12 metre area with 4 hoops. Goal: bounce-pass the ball into a hoop. Rules: no contact, three steps with the ball, every player touches the ball. Scoring: 1 point per hoop. Safety: bibs and no running into hoops. Adjustment: hoops can be moved closer.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Parts of a game design.","caption":"Include every part.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Design</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">name, goal, rules, scoring</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safety</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">no contact, bibs</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Adjusting a game for all.","caption":"Adjust so all can play.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Adjust</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">closer hoops</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Include</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lighter ball</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w24-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
